[
 {
  "verse_id": "chunk-v2-q-saptamo-k11-close-v31",
  "song": "chunk-v2-q-saptamo-k11-close",
  "verse": 31,
  "scope": "passage",
  "structured": true,
  "speaker": "Abhinavagupta (commentator voice)",
  "question": "The saptamo vimarśa closes with the question of the *omniscient* (sarvajña) — the\nsupreme knower who is the support of all the powers. The Buddhist objector presses: if the piśāca (the\ngoblin) is not perceived even by the omniscient, how can he deny it? The analysis turns on whether the\nomniscient's non-perception of the piśāca establishes its non-being, and how the ordinary knower\n(arvāgdarśana) differs. This grounds the omniscient as the one who knows all — the support of the\npowers.",
  "premises": [
   "Even if the piśāca is not seen by us, the form-and-the-rest atoms are there — not seen by us, but seen\nby the omniscient. The atoms have no un-precedented variety. The \"na tathā\" — the obstructing ones\nalso, not utterly. The \"dūrataram\" — the shadow, made by the darkness, with the interval, the\ndistinguished, the same-class-devoid interval; the middle-darkness or the middle-void. In their own\nplace, they withdraw; by the unable-moment-production-order, they are withdrawn. Hence the\npiśāca-atoms are, for the omniscient, equal to the darkness-and-the-rest-other-being-atoms. But if the\npiśāca is absent, then where the piśāca had occupied the interval, the āloka-atoms alone, being seen,\nare the object of the piśāca's-absence-dealing for the omniscient. The objection: \"seeing the other\nobjects, how does the omniscient deny the distinct piśāca?\" — in the piśāca's being-or-not-being, the\nown-form-standing of these is not lost; the distinct-piśāca's-absence is, by this way, hard to\nestablish even by the omniscient. The \"athavā\" (or): whatever is, is to be seen by him; then, there, if\nhe does not see the piśāca, it is not — and there, everything is the piśāca-included thing, known by\nanother cognition distinct from its being; or that very cognition is the object of the\npiśāca's-absence-dealing — so the interval-atom-tracing does nothing. The objection \"why is it not so\nfor us too?\" — answered (arvāk): \"he who sees the limited, for him there is no known\" — this\ncognition arises, by which the cognition is the cause of the being-included-cognition-marker\nnon-being-dealing. But where the certainty arises (like the smelling, the tasting, the attending, the\ntouching — the necessary-conceiving of the smell-cognition), there the smell-cognition's removal is\nthe cause of the non-being-dealing (the breath-entering-cognition-marker, the un-divided-from-the-\nsmell-taste-sound-touch-absence). Hence, though the piśāca's-absence is grasped by him who grasps the\nāloka or the place (by the piśāca's otherness), still it is the limited, grasped as if un-grasped (no\ncause of the removal of the conceiving of the distinct in the place or the āloka). The objection \"if\nthe piśāca's non-negatability's cause is the invisibility, then the jar's negatability's cause would\nbe the visibility (1.7.10, the 'svajñāna-maghaṭātmatā')\" — answered (siddham): \"every negation\nculminates in the identity-negation; and there the visibility is un-deviating (the hetubindu: the\ndistinction is to be said).\""
  ],
  "inference": "Decision for L2\nProceed. The argument is understood: the omniscient, seeing all the atoms, knows the piśāca's absence\nin its place (the āloka-atoms alone); the limited seer (arvāk) cannot — his non-perception is only the\nlimited, not the established non-being; and every negation culminates in the identity-negation, where\nthe visibility is un-deviating. The omniscient is the support of all the powers — the one who knows all,\nwhose non-perception establishes, whose perception is whole. The ||7|| colophon closes the saptamo\nvimarśa.\nEOF\necho \"V2Q map: $(wc -l < /mnt/HC_Volume_106427611/sanskritree/translations/_stack/ipvv/pilot/pilot_V2Q_ARGUMENT_MAP.md) lines\"",
  "uncertainties": [
   "The 1.7.10 (\"svajñāna-maghaṭātmatā\") and the hetubindu references — cited; the applications",
   "The \"arvāk\" (the limited-seeing) vs. the sarvajña distinction — the crux; kept close."
  ],
